# Architecture

Profile: `{{ profile }}`. This is a project-owned starting decision. Change the documented rules and their validator together when the domain needs a different structure.

{% if profile == 'typescript-web' %}
## Source map and dependency direction

| Path | Responsibility | Allowed source imports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `src/domain/` | Pure domain functions and types | `domain` only; no external package imports |
| `src/app/` | React UI and presentation behavior | `app`, `domain` |
| `src/infrastructure/` | Future browser/network/storage adapters | `infrastructure`, `domain` |
| `src/main.tsx` | Composition root: mounts the UI and wires future adapters | May compose the layers |
| `src/styles.css` | Application styles | No TypeScript imports |
| `tests/` | Behavior tests | May exercise the layers |

`App.tsx` calls `domain/greeting.ts`; the greeting function has no React dependency. Infrastructure is initially empty. When adding an adapter, pass its behavior into the UI from `main.tsx` rather than importing infrastructure directly into `app`. External packages are permitted in `app` and `infrastructure`.

`.project/validate.mjs` uses the TypeScript parser and resolver to check imports, re-exports, import types, and literal dynamic imports inside the three layers. Nonliteral dynamic loading, unknown root source files, and unknown source-layer directories fail. For example, `domain` importing React or `app` importing `infrastructure` fails `./project check`. The composition root is deliberately outside those layer restrictions.
{% else %}
## Source map and dependency direction

The package name is `{{ package_name }}` under `src/`.

| Path within the package | Responsibility | Allowed project imports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `domain/` | Domain functions and types | `domain` only |
| `application/` | Use cases that coordinate domain behavior | `application`, `domain` |
| `adapters/` | HTTP and future external-system adapters | `adapters`, `application`, `domain` |
| `main.py` | Composition root: creates FastAPI and mounts adapters | May compose the layers |
| `__init__.py` | Package identity; keep free of framework wiring | Domain-level restrictions |

`adapters/http.py` defines the health and greeting routes. Its greeting route calls `application/greeting.py`, which delegates to `domain/greeting.py`. Domain and application may use Python's standard library but cannot import third-party packages. FastAPI belongs in the HTTP adapter and composition root. `tests/` exercises domain behavior and the HTTP interface.

`.project/validate.py` parses Python imports, including relative imports, and checks these directions. Dynamic module-loading forms covered by the validator and unknown packages/layers fail. For example, `domain` importing FastAPI or `application` importing `adapters` fails `./project check`. `main.py` is deliberately outside those layer restrictions.
{% endif %}

## Enforcement and limits

`./project check` combines the boundary validator with metadata/task validation, type checks, profile-specific linting, and tests. The import policy catches supported static dependencies. It does not prove domain purity, prevent all possible runtime loading, or restrict operating-system capabilities. Review remains necessary for changes to composition roots, validators, dependencies, and behavior.

No database, deployment target, model provider, or agent runtime is assumed. Record a concrete need in `docs/decisions/` before adding a new architectural component. A task's allowed paths describe scope; they are not a filesystem permission mechanism.

## Ownership

The template seeds `src/`, `tests/`, this file, `README.md`, `docs/decisions/`, and `.project/tasks/` once. Copier excludes them from updates, including files you deleted. Contract tooling, schemas, and stack configuration can receive reviewed template updates; see [upgrading](upgrading.md). Keep this document and the executable boundary policy in agreement when changing the layout.
